Central permission manager (Phase 4)

One dashboard over nsIPermissionManager, so "what have I allowed, and where"
is a question with an answer instead of a site-by-site excavation. Ships as a
Site permissions group in the Privacy Center pane: every capability with a
count of exceptions, a global default, an expandable per-site list with the
grant state, per-site revoke, per-type clear, and clear-everything.

FINDING, and the reason this patch does not match the plan text: Firefox 152
does not persist clipboard access as a site permission at all.

REMAINING_WORK and privacy/README both name "camera, microphone, location,
notifications and clipboard". The first four exist as permission types with
permissions.default.<type> prefs in firefox.js. Clipboard has neither. There
is no "clipboard" or "clipboard-read" permission type anywhere in the pinned
Gecko, and no permissions.default.clipboard. A page that wants to read the
clipboard gets a one-time in-content Paste confirmation, and nothing is
recorded -- so there is no stored grant to list, audit or revoke.

A clipboard row was therefore not built. Rendering one would have shown the
user a control over state that does not exist, and a "0 sites" count that
would stay 0 forever no matter what they permitted. The pane says plainly why
the row is missing instead. That is a better outcome than a dashboard that
lies by omission, but the plan text should be corrected rather than left
looking half-implemented -- REMAINING_WORK is updated to match.

Since the four named types are a thin dashboard on their own, the table covers
the rest of Gecko's persisted, privacy-relevant permissions -- xr,
local-network, persistent-storage, midi, speaker-selection, autoplay-media.
"Instead of per-site digging" is only true if the dashboard is where the
digging actually ends.

Two design notes worth keeping:

  * The global default is the half that makes this a manager rather than a
    list. "Stop asking me about notifications" is one control here, instead of
    the same decision repeated per site forever. Types with no
    permissions.default.<type> pref get the per-site list and no selector,
    rather than a selector that would silently do nothing.
  * UNKNOWN_ACTION entries are filtered out. Gecko stores "no opinion" rows
    that are not exceptions the user made, and showing them would inflate
    every count with decisions nobody took.

The rows are generated in JS from KAVACHA_PERMISSION_TYPES rather than written
as markup, so the table and the UI cannot drift apart -- adding a capability
is one line. Expanded rows survive re-render, because perm-changed fires on
every removal and a list that collapsed under the user mid-cleanup would be
unusable. The pane observes perm-changed so counts stay honest while the
doorhanger or identity panel is changing permissions behind it.

Verified without a build:
  * kavacha-privacy.js passes node --check; the pane fragment parses as
    well-formed XML under the XUL + XHTML namespaces.
  * Every Fluent id referenced by the pane -- including the ten built by
    concatenation as "kavacha-permission-" + type -- resolves against
    zen-preferences.ftl. This check exists because D0e shipped FTL-less
    controls that measured correctly in every geometry probe and rendered
    blank; a missing string is invisible to everything except this.
  * The permission type strings and their default prefs were read out of the
    pinned tree (firefox.js:968-975), not assumed.

Not verified: behaviour against a live permission store -- that removeByType
empties the list, that the default selector changes what a site is asked, that
long origins ellipsize rather than pushing the controls off the pane. That
needs a build and a profile with real grants in it.
